---
import ToolLayout from '../../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
import HttpMethodsReference from '../../components/HttpMethodsReference.jsx';
---
<ToolLayout title="HTTP Methods Reference" description="Quick reference for HTTP methods — purpose, safety, idempotency, and body support.">
  <HttpMethodsReference client:load slot="tool" />

  <h2>Why idempotency matters more than method choice alone</h2>
  <p>Knowing whether a method is idempotent directly informs safe retry behavior — a network timeout on a GET can be retried freely, but retrying a non-idempotent POST blindly risks duplicate side effects like double-charging a payment.</p>

  <h2>How to use this reference</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Check which method matches your operation's semantics (read, create, replace, partial update, delete).</li>
    <li>Note its safety and idempotency properties when designing retry logic.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Common mistakes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Using GET for operations with side effects</strong> — GET should be safe (no state changes), and browsers/proxies may cache or prefetch GET requests, causing unintended repeated execution.</li>
    <li><strong>Assuming POST is always non-idempotent by necessity</strong> — with an idempotency key, a POST endpoint can be made safely retryable even though the method itself doesn't guarantee it.</li>
    <li><strong>Confusing PUT and PATCH</strong> — PUT replaces the entire resource; PATCH applies a partial update. Using PUT with partial data can unintentionally null out omitted fields.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Use cases</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Designing REST API endpoints with correct method semantics</li>
    <li>Deciding whether a failed request is safe to automatically retry</li>
    <li>Reviewing an API design for adherence to HTTP method conventions</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>FAQ</h2>
  <p><strong>Is PATCH always idempotent?</strong> Not necessarily — it depends on the specific update. A PATCH that sets an absolute value is idempotent; one that applies a relative change (like incrementing a counter) is not.</p>
</ToolLayout>
